course: dictionary
lesson: 713
title: One word, two meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. The context, meaning the words around the word, tells you which meaning is right. The word barge is a good example: it can be a boat, or it can be a verb meaning to push rudely past people.
explain: «The horse walked along the towpath beside the canal, pulling the boat by a long rope.» The clues are canal, towpath and rope, so barge here means a boat. A towpath is the path beside a canal, and a barge is a long, flat boat that carries heavy loads.
explain: Now think about barge as a verb. To barge in means to interrupt or enter rudely without being asked. If someone barges into your game without asking, they are not being polite, and the word tells you that. Try your meaning in the sentence to check it makes sense.
fact: A barge is a long boat with a flat bottom, used to carry heavy loads on rivers and canals.
fact: A canal barge is a barge that travels along a canal.
fact: Barge can also be a verb: to push roughly or rudely past people or into a place.
fact: Barge in means to interrupt or enter rudely without being asked.
fact: Barges carry things like coal, sand and goods, and a maths problem may ask how many tonnes a barge carries.
exercise: «The horse walked along the towpath beside the canal, pulling the boat by a long rope.» What is a barge in this sentence? | A rope for tying up boats | A rude person who pushes past | *A long, flat boat that carries heavy loads | A horse that pulls carts
exercise: Sam says, «Don't barge in while we are talking.» What does barge in mean? | To swim quickly across a river | *To interrupt or enter rudely without being asked | To wait patiently at the door | To carry heavy sand on a canal
exercise: «The barge-woman steers with the tiller.» What is a tiller? | A roller for squeezing water out of wet washing | A path beside the canal | *The long handle at the back used for steering | A rope that pulls the boat
test: Ali did not wait his turn and tried to barge past everyone in the queue. What does barge past mean here? | Float slowly along a river | Carry heavy sand to a boat | Wait patiently in line | *Push roughly and rudely past people
test: «The heavy coal was loaded onto the barge.» Which meaning of barge fits this sentence? | A small paddle boat for fun | To push rudely past people | *A long, flat boat that carries heavy loads | To interrupt without being asked
exam: Jayden asks, how can I tell which meaning of barge is right in a sentence? | Look only at the first letter of the word | Skip the word and never come back to it | Always choose the longest meaning | *Read the words around it and swap each meaning into the sentence to see which one makes sense
